y woman at the end  we mentioned in Europe that she could  become well-behaved I wonder was there  anything endearing or soft about this  woman or was it a show  I mean understanding a little bit about  the atheist leaders now and the  organization's it's a plethora of people  being shunned and splitting off and so  it seems more normal right to me so I  just wondering is that is this harshness  just part of a show or I think to a  large extent it was it was who was her  performance so that she would be noticed  but she was a pretty outspoken woman and  even those who liked her said that in  private she was pretty tough to be  around  she didn't tolerate fools well at all  and if you said something she didn't  agree with she let you know there was no  question on the other hand they say she  had a fabulous sense of humor and you  know could be fun to be around but those  people were pretty few and far between I  think the frustration came out you know  with her over the years now you  mentioned Europe I think one of the  reasons again I want to emphasize that I  believe that she was better behaved in  Europe is because she didn't have the  spotlight to misbehave they didn't care  that she was there so there's another  atheist group it just wasn't a big issue  in Europe one of the things that kind of  ignited her in the United States was  that she wherever she went the spotlight  was on her and she would seize it by  behaving the way she did I suppose she  could have engaged in some outrageous  behavior abroad but she just didn't it  wasn't there  in your reading of her Diaries and in  your discussion of that early period of  her life and her own comment about I  believe it was in what in a later period  of her life where she wondered to  herself why she always wanted to be  angry and and against something it  sounded as though you might be implying  that she was or portraying her as a  rebel with the cause by nature that that  was her sort of fundamental personality  bent and further that that perhaps more  than anything more than particular  rational beliefs might be what was  driving her and I just wonder if you  would comment further on that if that is  what you're implying in the book and if  she ever herself seemed to if that is  the case  did she ever herself seem to come to  some kind of an understanding about what  was making her tick in terms of why she  needed to be always against something to  thrive yeah clearly there was a  rebellious nature there and she knew it  I think to a certain extent anyone in  that situation you must have had moments  of loneliness when she wished she wasn't  the way that she was because she led I  mean there's a pretty desperate life oh  I didn't go into it but the two or three  years after the Murray case she had had  guards her house was stoned windows  broken tires slash her sons were beat up  William was thrown before a bus I mean  it was pretty tough going and I'm sure  that she had moments when she wished  things were different but I think she  she was either worked in to put herself  into a situation where she had nothing  else that she could do but fight back  and one could put a gendered  interpretation of what's going on but I  really do see this as a woman in the  early 50s  very bright very well educated made some  mistake  and never got over it and decided rather  than apologizing for it she was gonna  pull the system down around her I really  think that was a lot of the motivation  okay well thank you very much I  appreciate it  [Music]  Brian LeBeau is author of the Atheist  Madalyn Murray O'Hair it's published by  New York University Press on the web at  NYU press dot org next week on public  lives a new biography of Andrew Jackson  from
 

